---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Self-Employed Tax Guide UK 2026/27: Income Tax, NICs & Deadlines | wagecheck"
  description="Complete guide to self-employed tax in the UK for 2026/27. Covers income tax, Class 2 and Class 4 NICs, payments on account, expenses and self-assessment deadlines."
  breadcrumbName="Self-Employed Tax Guide"
>
  <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 via-white to-lime-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 lg:py-20">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.24em] text-emerald-700">Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
        Self-Employed Tax Guide UK <span class="font-display italic text-emerald-600">2026/27</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">
        Everything you need to know about income tax, National Insurance contributions, payments on account and self-assessment deadlines if you are self-employed in the UK.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">Last updated: September 2026 · Tax year 2026/27</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8 text-base leading-7 text-slate-700">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How self-employed tax works</h2>
        <p>
          When you are self-employed, you do not have an employer deducting tax from your wages. Instead, you are responsible for reporting your income and paying tax through Self Assessment. You must register as self-employed with HMRC, keep records of your income and expenses, and file a tax return each year.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tax year runs from 6 April to 5 April. For 2026/27, the Personal Allowance is £12,570 — the same as for employees. Income tax rates are also the same: 20% on income between £12,571 and £50,270, 40% on income between £50,271 and £125,140, and 45% above that. Scottish taxpayers pay different rates through Revenue Scotland.
        </p>
        <p>
          The key difference from employment is National Insurance. Self-employed people pay Class 2 and Class 4 NICs instead of Class 1. These are calculated through your tax return rather than deducted at source by an employer.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Class 2 vs Class 4 NICs</h2>
        <p>Self-employed National Insurance comes in two parts:</p>
        <div class="my-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Class 2 NICs</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">A flat weekly contribution of £3.45 for 2026/27 (pro-rated to £179.40 per year). Class 2 NICs count towards your State Pension and other contributory benefits. If your profits are below £6,725 you can voluntarily pay Class 2 to protect your pension record, but you are not required to.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Class 4 NICs</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">A percentage of your trading profits: 6% on profits between £12,570 and £50,270, then 2% above £50,270. Class 4 NICs are paid through your Self Assessment tax return alongside your income tax.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Payment on account explained</h2>
        <p>
          If your last tax bill was more than £1,000, HMRC requires you to make "payments on account" — advance payments towards next year's tax bill. These are calculated as 50% of your previous year's liability and paid in two instalments: 31 January and 31 July.
        </p>
        <p>
          For example, if your 2025/26 tax bill was £6,000, you would pay £3,000 on 31 January 2027 and £3,000 on 31 July 2027 as payments on account for 2026/27. When you file your 2026/27 return, any balance is due by 31 January 2028.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your income has dropped, you can apply to reduce your payments on account through your Personal Tax Account. But be careful — reducing them too much results in interest charges from HMRC if your actual bill is higher.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What expenses can I claim?</h2>
        <p>
          One of the main advantages of self-employment is deducting business expenses from your turnover. Common allowable expenses include:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Office costs</strong> — rent, utilities, stationery, phone and internet (business portion).</li>
          <li><strong>Travel</strong> — fuel, train fares, hotel stays for business trips. Home-to-office commuting is not allowable.</li>
          <li><strong>Equipment</strong> — laptops, tools, software. Items over £1,000 are typically capitalised and claimed as capital allowances.</li>
          <li><strong>Marketing</strong> — website costs, advertising, business cards.</li>
          <li><strong>Professional fees</strong> — accountant fees, solicitor costs, professional indemnity insurance.</li>
          <li><strong>Training</strong> — courses directly related to your current trade.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You can also claim the simplified expenses flat rate if you work from home: £10/month for 25–50 hours, £18/month for 51–100 hours, or £26/month for 101+ hours. Alternatively, you can calculate the actual proportion of your household bills used for business.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Self-assessment deadlines</h2>
        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 text-left text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500">
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Date</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">What happens</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">5 October</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Deadline to register as self-employed for a new business</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">31 October</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Paper tax return deadline (rarely used now)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">31 January</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Online tax return deadline + pay any tax owed + first payment on account</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">31 July</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Second payment on account</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Filing late incurs an immediate £100 penalty, with further daily charges if you are more than three months late. Paying late incurs interest at 2.5% on the outstanding amount. HMRC penalties escalate significantly the longer you delay.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Registering as self-employed</h2>
        <p>
          You must register with HMRC by 5 October after the end of the tax year in which you started self-employment. For example, if you started freelancing in July 2026, you must register by 5 October 2026.
        </p>
        <p>
          Register online at <a href="https://www.gov.uk/register-for-self-assessment" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700 font-semibold">GOV.UK</a>. You will receive a Unique Taxpayer Reference (UTR) number within about 10 working days. Keep this number safe — you need it to file your tax return.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once registered, you should also set up a separate bank account for your business income and expenses. This makes bookkeeping much simpler and helps you avoid mixing personal and business finances.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Self-Employed Tax Calculator</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          Use our <a href="/self-employed-tax-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">self-employed tax calculator</a> to work out your income tax, Class 2 and Class 4 NICs, and total tax bill for 2026/27. Enter your trading profits and expenses to see a clear breakdown.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
